steps.body:
An `if` runs its block only when the condition is true. Add `else` to say what happens otherwise. The `else` has no condition of its own: it catches every case the `if` did not.

```java
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Adult");
} else {
    System.out.println("Minor");
}
```

Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never both and never neither. That makes `else` the right tool for a yes or no decision.

Two gotchas:

- Never put a semicolon right after the condition. `if (x > 0);` ends the `if` with an empty statement, so the block below it always runs.
- To test for odd, write `n % 2 != 0`, not `n % 2 == 1`. For a negative odd number the remainder is `-1`, so the second form says `-7` is not odd.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 7;
        if (number % 2 == 0) {
            System.out.println(number + " is even");
        } else {
            System.out.println(number + " is odd");
        }

        number = 10;
        if (number % 2 == 0) {
            System.out.println(number + " is even");
        } else {
            System.out.println(number + " is odd");
        }
    }
}

