public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int balance = 50;
        int price = 30;
        if (price <= balance) {
            System.out.println("Purchase " + price + ": covered, " + (balance - price) + " left");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Purchase " + price + ": not covered, " + (price - balance) + " missing");
        }

        price = 80;
        if (price <= balance) {
            System.out.println("Purchase " + price + ": covered, " + (balance - price) + " left");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Purchase " + price + ": not covered, " + (price - balance) + " missing");
        }
    }
}

steps.body:
When there are more than two outcomes, chain them with `else if`. Java tests each condition from top to bottom. The first one that is true runs its block, and the rest of the chain is skipped. The final `else` runs only when nothing above matched.

```java
if (speed > 120) {
    System.out.println("Ticket");
} else if (speed > 100) {
    System.out.println("Warning");
} else {
    System.out.println("OK");
}
```

Because the first true branch wins, order matters. Put the most specific case first, which for `>` and `>=` tests means the highest threshold. With `speed > 100` on top, a speed of 130 would only get a warning, because the ticket check is never reached.

Each `else if` can rely on everything above it being false. Below `speed > 120`, you already know `speed <= 120`, so you do not need to write `speed > 100 && speed <= 120`.

Separate `if` statements are not the same thing. Each one is checked on its own, so several of them can run.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int score = 95;
        if (score >= 90) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": A");
        } else if (score >= 80) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": B");
        } else if (score >= 70) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": C");
        } else if (score >= 60) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": D");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": F");
        }

        score = 72;
        if (score >= 90) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": A");
        } else if (score >= 80) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": B");
        } else if (score >= 70) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": C");
        } else if (score >= 60) {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": D");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Score " + score + ": F");
        }
    }
}

